> Is the problem this, or that it's too easy to obtain the "killer weapons"?=


For non-artifact weapons there isn't as much difference class to
class.  If you're going for an artifact-free challenge game and you
end up with a blessed +5 rustproof mace, go for it.  High enchantment
matters more than type to type weapon difference.  The difference
between a +0 katana and a +0 mace gets lost in the noise by the
time it's a blessed rustproof +5 mace.

Reading the damage by weapon type and the weight by weapon
type, it turns out the two-handed weapons hit harder than the
one-handed weapons and none of the damages per weight are
surprising.  In a game with no artifacts the weapons classes and
skill system works very well IMO.  Except that silver saber and
scimitar are different classes but silver dagger and orcish dagger
are in the same class ...

Basically what I've taken from the extensive discussion of missile
combat over the years is the skills system works very well when
artifacts are not taken into account.

But with artifacts being so much better than other weapons,
there's a desire for specific artifacts and those skill classes become
far more valuable.  The difference is so great I might use Greyswandir
even if I'm restricted in saber.

Artifacts greatly skew a view of the skills system.  I'm not sure
that's a problem.  Who uses artifacts other than melee?  ;^)


